1 NAME Carolina /Tufvesdotter/
2 GIVN Carolina
2 SURN Tufvesdotter
2 _MARNM Johnsson
1 SEX F
1 BURI
2 ADDR
3 STAE Colorado
3 CTRY United States
1 BIRT
2 DATE 18 MAR 1846
2 ADDR
3 CITY Vedby
3 STAE Hyllstofta 7
1 DEAT
2 DATE 13 AUG 1890
2 ADDR
3 STAE Colorado
3 CTRY United States
1 NOTE {geni:about_me}      
2 CONT Carolina was born the 18th of Mar. 1846. Sometime before the birth of her first child in 1871, she and two of her sisters, Cecelia, and Eva, and her younger brother Olaf, came to America. She died Aug 13, 1890 at age 44.
2 CONT The children born to Magnus and Carolina were Anna 1871, Emil 1873, who died in 1884, Ellen born in 1875, Carl John, born in 1877, Gustaf Filomen in 1880, and Selma born in 1883.
2 CONT Carolina was born in Rya, Kristianstad, Skane, Sweden. Her parents were JohanTuvesson, and Elna Eliasdotter. She had four sisters and three brothers, one brother died at the age of six. The family went through all the adversities 
2 CONC of nature, that settling on the plains cost......drought, hail, prairie fire, plagues of grasshoppers....but they prospered, on the plains of Kansas. With the parents, Magnus and Carolina dying as young as they did, and Gustaf's d
2 CONC eath at only 16, with tuberculosis, makes us think the parents had tuberculosis also. The high, dry climate of Colorado made it a mecca for Tuberculosis patients, and Ellen was sent with Gustaf to the Colorado Springs area. She wo
2 CONC rked to help pay for his board and room at the sanitarium. There Ellen met Edward Dwyer, who was a handyman at the place. Gus was brought back to Colorado, again but he did not recover his health. My mother says she remembers when
2 CONC  her Mother, Ellen, and her sister Anna, took Gustaf to the train, they cried all the way there and back, seeming to know they would not see him again.
2 CONT I don't know when the Johnsons' moved to Colorado. Ellen and Edward Dwyer were married in 1901. It seems that after Gustaf's death they moved.
2 CONT Anna married a man by the name of Bob Moore, and Selma married Han Olsen Saue, and John married Edith Beckwith.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Written by Eleanor Barber 
2 CONT In the province of Skane, southernmost province of Sweden, our ancestors lived. They owned the same property for as long as we have records, which are in the early 1700's. They were prosperous, as the landowners mostly were, but t
2 CONC hey earned some moneys from the keeping of horses for the cavalry, and this continued for several generations. Our third great Uncle, Olaf Johansson, was able to come to the United States, and bring three of his nieces, and purcha
2 CONC se land in Kansas. We do not know if he homesteaded, but he purchased a sizable amount, as he had his own farm, and his oldest niece and her husband lived on the adjoining farm, which he had orignally owned. The farms were excepti
2 CONC onal for their time, beautiful with trees, lawns and even a tennis court, and having fine stock, and excellent riding horses, and dray horses.The reason for their emigration from Sweden, was health. The area corresponding to our c
2 CONC ounties, called Kristiaastad, where they lived was in northwest Skane. It's climate is one of constant winter dampness, months on end without sun, and if not rain then mist, and cloud. In the summer there is much rainfall, and the
2 CONC  air is always humid. In this kind of climate tuberculosis was a constant threat. In the stories of early Sweden people, especially it seemed the women, lived very short lives, and the cause of death was largely tuberculosis. Grap
2 CONC hic descriptions of this are given in the books by Sigrid Uadset. So the Johnson's sought relief from this scourge; but did not come far enough West, and it still took it's toll of the family, as the eldest daughter, our second gr
2 CONC eat grandmother died in her forties, as did her husband, and at least two of her sons. Then Uncle Olaf brought the remaining family of our great great grandparents to Colorado, where they were able to live lives free from this dre
2 CONC ad affliction.
