1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Biographical and Genealogical Reconstruction Based on the Court Records of the Turku Magistrate's Court and the Lawman's Court of Nyland and Tavastehus: Henrik Wilhelm Hellström (1809–1869)
2 CONT
2 CONT This summary reconstructs the life events, social networks, and financial activities of the vicar, rural dean, and legal actor Henrik Wilhelm Hellström (28 November 1809 – 8 October 1869) in 19th-century Turku, Karis, and Vihti. T
2 CONC he court records of the Magistrate's Court, the Chamber Court, the District Courts, and the Lawman's Court, along with the minutes of notification matters, portray not only a diligent pastor but also an active financial manager, l
2 CONC ender, real estate investor, and legal agent who handled assignments of trust for the Cathedral Chapter. His life reflects the multifaceted role of the 19th-century clergy as the spiritual, financial, and judicial leader of their 
2 CONC community.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family Relations, Posthumous Birth, and Early Years in Turku
2 CONT
2 CONT Henrik Wilhelm Hellström was born in Turku (Åbo) on 28 November 1809, the son of the Turku carpenter Henrik Hellström (1767–1809) and his second wife, Ester Jakobsdotter. The most significant genealogical and life-shaping factor w
2 CONC as his posthumous birth, i.e., after his father's death. Henrik Hellström had passed away just over a month earlier, on 6 October 1809, at only 42 years of age, from "nerve fever" (nerffeb). Consequently, the widow Ester was pregn
2 CONC ant while navigating the difficult probate and debt settlement process described in the Magistrate's Court records of 1810–1811. According to the court records, Ester Hellström applied in the spring of 1810 for permission to sell 
2 CONC a dilapidated inherited property in the Kloster quarter (kloster qvarteret, No. 136), as the estate's debts (over 113 riksdaler) far exceeded its assets (72 riksdaler). The court ordered that the closest relatives be consulted, as
2 CONC  the sale also affected the inheritance share of the minor son (the newborn Henrik Wilhelm). Thus, Henrik Wilhelm was born into a situation of debt and legal uncertainty, which likely influenced his later meticulousness in financi
2 CONC al and legal matters.
2 CONT
2 CONT The known siblings and half-siblings on his parents' side confirm the family's broader networks. His full siblings included at least Henrik Johan Hellström (from his father's third marriage? more precise research would require par
2 CONC ish records) and, on his mother's side, Johan Fredric Asplund, which suggests that the widow Ester later remarried a man from the Asplund family. This partly explains why Henrik Wilhelm later served as a guardian and handled exten
2 CONC sive family-related legal matters.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marital Status, Spouse, and Descendants
2 CONT
2 CONT Henrik Wilhelm Hellström married Vilhelmina Sofia Hellström (née Printz). The union produced several children, of whom the court records and genealogical data mention at least Emil Vilhelm Hellström, Rosina Antoinette Gadolin (who
2 CONC  apparently married into the Gadolin family), Henrik Theodor Hellström, Aurora Wilhelmina Hellström, Sofia Adelaide Hellström, Emilia Henriette Procopé, and Axel Adolf Hellström. In particular, the marriage of his daughter Rosina 
2 CONC Antoinette to the Gadolin family indicates Hellström's successful social ascent within the bourgeoisie and clergy, as the noble Gadolin family was known for its academic and ecclesiastical influence.
2 CONT
2 CONT Career and Financial Management in Karis (c. 1840s–1850s)
2 CONT
2 CONT Hellström's theological career likely began in the 1830s, and he was ordained as a priest. His first known clerical posts were in Karis (Karis), where he served as a chaplain (vice pastor, kapellan). During his tenure, he lived in
2 CONC  the Kihla chaplain's residence, which had fallen into severe disrepair under his predecessor, Chaplain Erik Hamberg. Hellström immediately became embroiled in a dispute over the condition of the parsonage, leading to a lengthy le
2 CONC gal process in the District Court and the Lawman's Court (1845–1847). He appealed the decision of the transfer inspection (husesyn), as the estimated repair costs amounted to as much as 1,237 rubles 71 kopecks, and he demanded tha
2 CONC t the parish be held responsible, as his predecessor's estate had not maintained the property.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Karis, Hellström was responsible for managing the parish's grain and poor funds. The court records from 1843 to 1852 depict him as a diligent debt collector. He enforced several promissory notes, such as the loans of miller Car
2 CONC l Fredrik Strömberg (20 rubles, 1843) and crofter Fredrik Savonius (175 rubles, 1844). A particularly significant case was the legal dispute in 1847 against the estate of the sexton (klockare) Carl Björkman: Hellström, acting as c
2 CONC hurch warden (kyrkovärd), demonstrated through an audit that Björkman's estate owed 140 rubles 54 ½ kopecks to the parish funds and pursued the claim in the District Court. In these tasks, he acted as an uncompromising supervisor 
2 CONC of the parish's assets.
2 CONT
2 CONT Activities as a Representative of the Chapter and the Crown
2 CONT
2 CONT Henrik Wilhelm Hellström frequently served in trust assignments for the Turku Cathedral Chapter (consistorium) and the Crown (krono). He is mentioned in the Lawman's Court records of 1845–1848 as the Chapter's representative in, a
2 CONC mong other things, the financial inspections of the Kyrkslätt parsonage and at the Karislojo courts. In 1848, he acted as the Chapter's authorized representative in the Lawman's Court regarding the condition of Provost Palmberg's 
2 CONC parsonage. This demonstrates that he enjoyed broad trust in both ecclesiastical and secular administration, and his legal expertise was widely recognised.
2 CONT
2 CONT Transition to Vicar of Vihti, Real Estate Holdings, and Final Years (1860s)
2 CONT
2 CONT In the late 1850s or early 1860s, Hellström moved from Karis to become the vicar and provost of the Vihti (Wichtis) parish. The court records from his time in Vihti (1864–1869) describe him as an active landowner and local influen
2 CONC cer. In 1864, he purchased three-quarters of the Lillkas tax farm (Lillkas skatteaugments hemman) in the village of Nuhala, Vihti, for 16,000 marks, and received a legal title (lainhuuto) from the District Court after three public
2 CONC  proclamations. That same year, he appears as a witness and party in several court cases, such as disputes concerning the maintenance of fences and ditches. While serving as chairman of Vihti's poor fund, he was involved in 1866 i
2 CONC n resolving a complex testamentary matter concerning the inheritance of Hedvig Löfman, the widow of the deceased tailor Jöns Löfqvist, whose heirs were partly unknown.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hellström died in Vihti on 8 October 1869 at the age of 59. He was buried in the Vihti parish cemetery. At the Vihti winter assizes in 1867, he still conducted the divine service, and the court minutes mention him as an authoritat
2 CONC ive validator of witnesses' competence right up until his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Financial Activities, Trust Assignments, and Ties to the Wider Network
2 CONT
2 CONT Hellström was an exceptionally financially active clergyman. He not only managed parish funds but also lent and borrowed money, purchased real estate, and acted as a guardian. The 500-riksdaler loan he took out in Turku in 1830, t
2 CONC he tens of rubles in debts he collected in Karis, and the 16,000-mark land purchase in Vihti all demonstrate considerable wealth and business acumen. In addition, he acted as guardian for several estates and minors, for example, i
2 CONC n 1853, when the Pussi tax farm in Karis was sold, he served as the guardian of the widow and daughters of the late Crown Bailiff Fredrik Printz.
2 CONT
2 CONT Genealogical Significance and Connection to Earlier Generations
2 CONT
2 CONT The story of Henrik Wilhelm Hellström is an exceptional example of early 19th-century social mobility. From a bourgeois and artisan background in Turku (his grandfather Matts Hellström was a tobacco roller, his father Henrik Hells
2 CONC tröm a carpenter), he rose through his clerical career to become a respected member of the clergy in Uusimaa. His posthumous birth and his mother Ester's perseverance through debt crises partly explain his lifelong precision in fi
2 CONC nancial matters. The marriage of his daughter Rosina Antoinette to the Gadolin family reinforces the family's connection to academic and ecclesiastical circles of influence. Although no personal letters from Hellström have survive
2 CONC d, the extensive testimonial evidence from court records paints a portrait of a vigorous, legally astute, and financially skilled clerical leader who mastered the bureaucracy and legal culture of his time and left a lasting mark o
2 CONC n the history of both the Karis and Vihti parishes.
2 CONT
