1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Biographical and Genealogical Reconstruction Based on Magistrate and Court Records, Newspapers, and Genealogical Source Materials: Axel Adolf Hellström (1849–1925)
2 CONT
2 CONT This reconstruction assembles the life events, social networks, financial activities, and public position of industrialist, brewery entrepreneur, and Vaasa municipal politician Axel Adolf Hellström (23 February 1849 – 28 October 1
2 CONC 925) during the turn of the 19th and 20th centuries in Vyborg, Koivisto, Vaasa, and Sibbo. Magistrate and court records, moving certificates, newspapers, company histories, and the university matriculation register portray an exce
2 CONC ptionally energetic and multi-faceted businessman who amassed his wealth through the brewing industry, real estate, and trust assignments, but who was also repeatedly brought before the courts in both commercial and personal matte
2 CONC rs. His career reflects the diverse field of activity of the bourgeoisie in industrialising Finland at the end of the 19th century: a combination of brewing entrepreneurship, real estate investment, municipal politics, and banking
2 CONC  activities that was characteristic of the urban bourgeoisie of the era but unusually extensive for a single individual.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family Relations, Birth, and Early Years in Karis
2 CONT
2 CONT Axel Adolf Hellström was born in Kihla, Karis, on 23 February 1849 (baptised 25 February), the youngest son of the vicar and rural dean Henrik Wilhelm Hellström (1809–1869) and his wife Vilhelmina Sofia Printz (1816–1892). Due to 
2 CONC his father's clerical career, the family resided at the Kihla chaplain's residence, as attested by communion books from 1846 to 1855. Axel Adolf was the seventh child of the family; known siblings included Rosina Antoinette, who m
2 CONC arried into the Gadolin family; Emilia Henriette, who married into the Procopé family; Aurora Wilhelmina; Emil Vilhelm; Henrik Theodor; and Sofia Adelaide. The early death of his father in October 1869, only four months before Axe
2 CONC l Adolf's matriculation, left the family under the mother's guardianship, but the family's substantial capital and extensive kinship networks secured the children's future. When the estate was divided in 1874, the Linkas tax farm 
2 CONC in Vihti was sold, and Axel Adolf acted as one of the sellers together with his mother and siblings, as evidenced by both the deed of sale and the power of attorney that have been preserved. This inheritance, although not a direct
2 CONC  catalyst for business activities, likely provided financial security and social capital that enabled the transition from an academic career to industrial entrepreneurship.
2 CONT
2 CONT Education and Transition to the Brewing Industry
2 CONT
2 CONT Axel Adolf matriculated at the University of Helsinki on 15 May 1869 (jur.yo), just weeks before his father's death, but did not complete a degree. Instead, he entered the brewing industry, which was a rapidly growing sector in la
2 CONC te 19th-century Finland. From 1875 to 1886, he served as production manager (brewer) at the Koivisto Brewery (Koivisto Bryggeri, P. Koch & Co), located in Tervahartiala, Koivisto rural municipality, on the Karelian Isthmus. The Ko
2 CONC ivisto brewery was one of Finland's early industrial breweries, and Hellström's position there signified a professional specialisation that was still uncommon at the time. The expertise in brewing that he acquired in Koivisto form
2 CONC ed the foundation for his subsequent business career.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage, Family, and Moves from Eastern Finland to Ostrobothnia
2 CONT
2 CONT He moved to Vyborg on 6 August 1877 (notification 25 June) and from there further to Nurmijärvi (Hyvinkää) on 1 August 1877. On 26 August 1877, he married Antonia Ingeborg Bergman, daughter of an innkeeper from Hyvinkää. The marri
2 CONC age was solemnised in Nurmijärvi and signified Hellström's affiliation with a Swedish-speaking bourgeois family, which likely facilitated his business activities. On 20 September 1878, the couple moved to Koivisto, where Axel cont
2 CONC inued his work in the brewery and where several children were born to the family. From his marriage to Antonia Ingeborg, nine children were born: Thekla Ingeborg (later Buss), Thure Adolf, Walborg (later von Kothen), Aksel Olof, T
2 CONC orborg Judith, Judith Maria, Thyra Ester (later Hartman), Moritz Wilhelm, and Fredrik Sigurd. The marriages of his descendants into the von Kothen and Buss families demonstrate the family's integration into the Swedish-speaking bo
2 CONC urgeoisie and nobility – von Kothen was an old Finnish noble family, while the Buss family was a well-known merchant family from Ekenäs. This social ascent continued from Axel Adolf to his children and is consistent with the three
2 CONC -generation upward trajectory of the Hellström family: grandfather Henrik Hellström was a carpenter, father Henrik Wilhelm rose to become a clergyman and rural dean, and Axel Adolf in turn became an industrial entrepreneur and cit
2 CONC y councillor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Business Activities and Real Estate Investment in Vaasa
2 CONT
2 CONT In August 1886, the family moved to Nikolaistad, i.e. Vaasa. There, Axel Adolf acquired the Sandviks Öl- och Porterbryggeri (Sandviks Brewery) and served as its owner from 1886 to 1895. Sandviks Bryggeri was one of Vaasa's oldest 
2 CONC breweries, and Hellström's acquisition signified both an expansion of business operations and a transition from brewery employee to factory owner. Later, he founded a soft drink factory (Hietalahti Soft Drink Factory), which howev
2 CONC er ceased operations in July 1910 – the announcement in the Vaasa newspaper of 30 July 1910 reports that Hellström informed the municipal finance committee that he was closing the said brewery and simultaneously requested permissi
2 CONC on to convert the existing factory building into a residential building. This indicates not only the termination of the business but also that Hellström had been able to invest in an industrial property that he wished to continue 
2 CONC to utilise for residential purposes. The magistrate's minutes from 1899 document a fire at Sandviks Bryggeri during the night of 19 March 1899. The fire destroyed the cellar building, and the insurance companies – Städernas Allmän
2 CONC na Brandstodsbolag, Skåne, and Pohjola – compensated the losses. Hellström reported the insurance values as 2,021 marks for the building and 19,000 marks for the movable property, demonstrating a significant capital commitment to 
2 CONC the industrial property. The fire and its compensation were typical industrial risks of the era, but Hellström's ability to manage them through insurance indicates financial acumen. The magistrate's minutes from 1890 to 1899 depic
2 CONC t Hellström as an active applicant for liquor licenses – he repeatedly applied for and obtained rights in several Vaasa properties, including buildings No 31, No 44, No 50, and No 29 on Handelsesplanaden and on Stora långgatan. Th
2 CONC e magistrate granted the licenses subject to certain conditions, such as restrictions on market days and evening hours, indicating that the authorities closely monitored alcohol service and that Hellström complied with these regul
2 CONC ations. In the court of first instance in 1907, Hellström and his wife Antonia took a loan of 5,000 marks from the Wasa Svenska Lyceum fund and obtained a mortgage on their property at No 50 Handelsesplanaden, demonstrating that H
2 CONC ellström utilised his real estate assets as a financial instrument.
2 CONT
2 CONT Legal Disputes and Controversies
2 CONT
2 CONT In the Vyborg court of first instance in 1876, Axel Adolf was a defendant in a case in which a railway worker, Johan Suhonen, accused him of assault. Several witnesses were heard during the trial, but as neither party was able to 
2 CONC substantiate their claims, the claims were dismissed. However, the parties were ordered to compensate the witnesses for their expenses: Suhonen was to pay his witnesses 6 marks and 2.50 marks, while Hellström was to pay his witnes
2 CONC s 2.50 marks. Both parties expressed dissatisfaction with the verdict, suggesting that the dispute was personal and acrimonious in nature – possibly Hellström's position as an employer or entrepreneur provoked resentment. In a tes
2 CONC tamentary matter in 1905, he is mentioned as a party when a copy of the mutual will of Ilmar and Selina Hornborg was delivered to him – the certificate is dated 6 May 1905. This suggests that Hellström also acted as a legal agent 
2 CONC or heir for his close circle, further reinforcing the image of a reliable and legally astute businessman.
2 CONT
2 CONT Public Trust Assignments and Final Years
2 CONT
2 CONT Axel Adolf Hellström was a member of the Vaasa City Council for several years in the early 20th century, and newspapers mention him as an active municipal politician. He also served as an inspector of the distillery and as a membe
2 CONC r of the board of the Vaasa Savings Bank in the 1910s. This indicates not only his public esteem but also that he had been able to establish a position among the local elite. In addition, he was the manager of the Kronan yeast fac
2 CONC tory in Vaasa, demonstrating that his expertise in the brewing industry extended to related industrial sectors, such as yeast production – yeast was a key raw material for breweries, so the transition was natural. In 1919, the fam
2 CONC ily moved to Sibbo (Nikkilä, Nickby Svärdfelts), where Axel Adolf spent his final years. He died in Ekenäs on 28 October 1925 at the age of 76 and was buried in Ekenäs Gamla Begravningsplats (the old cemetery of Ekenäs).
2 CONT
2 CONT Genealogical Significance and Connection to Earlier Generations
2 CONT
2 CONT Genealogically, Axel Adolf Hellström represents the bourgeois ascent of the late 19th century: from a clergyman's son he became an industrial entrepreneur, municipal politician, and banking figure, and he succeeded in preserving a
2 CONC nd increasing the family's wealth and social standing. The lawsuits, fires, liquor license applications, and loans – all these documents paint a picture of a vigorous, risk-tolerant, and legally skilled businessman who mastered th
2 CONC e rules of the urban culture and industry of his time. Although some of his ventures, such as the soft drink factory, ended in closure, he retained his position and trust assignments until his death. The marriages of his descendan
2 CONC ts into the von Kothen and Buss families reinforce the family's integration into the Swedish-speaking bourgeoisie and nobility, which is consistent with the three-generation upward trajectory of the Hellström family: grandfather M
2 CONC atts Hellström was a tobacco roller, his son Henrik Hellström a carpenter, father Henrik Wilhelm rose to become a clergyman and rural dean, and Axel Adolf in turn became an industrial entrepreneur, city councillor, and bank direct
2 CONC or. His story is not only a genealogical account but also a contemporary depiction of the transformation of Finnish industrialisation and urbanisation – a time when new professions and business opportunities opened up for clergyme
2 CONC n's sons who dared to exchange theology books for brewing equipment and city council minutes.
2 CONT
